No strace. Let me use the SecurityManager approach with Java 21: `-Djava.security.manager=allow` plus a custom policy denying all SocketPermission connect and a System.setSecurityManager call... Actually the app doesn't install a security manager, and installing one at JVM start via `-Djava.security.manager=<class>` — in Java 21, `-Djava.security.manager=allow` just permits programmatic installation. You can also specify `-Djava.security.manager=default` to install the default SM with a policy file: `-Djava.security.policy=/tmp/deny.policy`. In Java 18+, `-Djava.security.manager=default` installs the default security manager using the policy; combined with `-Djava.security.policy=...` which *appends*... Actually `java.security.policy` appends to the default policy; to override entirely use `==` (double equals) in the file path: `-Djava.security.policy==/tmp/deny.policy` means "use only this policy".

So: `-Djava.security.manager=default -Djava.security.policy==/tmp/deny-all-net.policy` where policy grants all permissions except we can't easily express "all except network". A policy granting `java.security.AllPermission` would allow network. Instead grant a curated set... that's fiddly because the JVM needs many permissions.

Alternative simpler approach: install a SecurityManager that only throws on network operations. Write a small Java launcher class in /tmp that installs a SecurityManager overriding checkConnect to throw, then invokes the app's main. With `-Djava.security.manager=allow`, `System.setSecurityManager` works in Java 21 (deprecated but functional). The SM's checkConnect throwing SecurityException → proves whether the app touches the network.

That's a decent, quick test. Let's do it:

```java
import java.security.Permission;

public class OfflineCheck {
    static class NetBan extends SecurityManager {
        @Override public void checkConnect(String host, int port) { throw new SecurityException("NETWORK BLOCKED: connect " + host + ":" + port); }
        @Override public void checkConnect(String host, int port, Object ctx) { throw new SecurityException("NETWORK BLOCKED: connect " + host + ":" + port); }
        @Override public void checkPermission(Permission perm) { /* allow all else */ }
        @Override public void checkPermission(Permission perm, Object context) { /* allow all else */ }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.setSecurityManager(new NetBan());
        try {
            Class<?> c = Class.forName("com.acme.tmpl.TemplateConvertApp");
            c.getMethod("main", String[].class).invoke(null, (Object) new String[0]);
            System.out.println("OFFLINE-CHECK: completed with zero network connect() calls");
        } finally {
            System.setSecurityManager(null);
        }
    }
}
```

Careful: checkPermission allowing all is fine; but System.setSecurityManager(null) later requires RuntimePermission "setSecurityManager" — allowed since checkPermission allows all.

Note: SecurityManager is deprecated in 21 → javac warnings; and `System.setSecurityManager` may print a warning at runtime. Also, in Java 21 the SM is deprecated for removal but still works unless `-Djava.security.manager=disallow`. Default is "allow"? In JDK 18+, the default is that setSecurityManager throws UnsupportedOperationException unless `-Djava.security.manager=allow` is specified. So pass `-Djava.security.manager=allow`.

Also DNS resolution goes through InetAddress → checkConnect is called for InetAddress.getAllByName? Actually the SM check for DNS is `checkConnect(host, -1)`. Yes, InetAddress resolution triggers checkConnect.

Also note: the app's working directory matters — run from /testbed/tmpl-svc so it finds data/ and writes out/. But out/conversion.log would be rewritten identically. That's fine (idempotent). Actually to avoid touching the deliverable, I can run it from /tmp copy? Simpler: run from /testbed/tmpl-svc — output identical, and it doubles as an idempotency check. But the cwd must contain data/tenant-config.txt, otherwise it falls back to the default template and out/conversion.log would be overwritten with a *different* (fallback) content! That would corrupt the deliverable.

